/E (STM was initially used to study the topology and atomic structure of surfaces of different metals. It allowed researchers to discern the atomic-scale properties of materials, including surface roughness, defects, and surface reaction pathways. But over the years, STM began to be employed for a variety of applications outside of atomic-scale imaging. It has been used to assemble and manipulate individual atoms on a surface, leading to advances in nanotechnology, such as the generation of nanostructures like quantum corrals and molecular switches. Additionally, STM can be used to construct contacts on nanodevices by depositing metals \(e.g., gold, silver, tungsten etc\) in specific patterns. Researchers have also used STM to trigger chemical reactions and study the resulting reaction mechanisms at the molecular level. Since its discovery,  the  STM  has  been  responsible  for  big )
/Alt ()
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
