/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Nature has preserved the Sun's magnetic history in cosmogenic isotopes like Beryllium-10 in polar ice cores and Carbon-14 in tree rings. When the Sun is magnetically active, faster solar wind sweeps away cosmic particles, reducing the deposition of these isotopes on Earth. Therefore, higher concentrations of these radio-isotopes in certain periods in the past indicate times of weaker solar activity and vice-versa. Harnessing this causal correlation, modern science in the 21st century has invented sophisticated techniques to reconstruct the Sun's magnetic cycles, tracing them back over the past 11,000 years [3]. Maunder minimum was not the only time when the Sun went silent; the reconstruction says our host star may have undergone such grand minimum phases for more than 20 times in the past 11 millennia!)
/E (Nature has preserved the Sun's magnetic history in cosmogenic isotopes like Beryllium-10 in polar ice cores and Carbon-14 in tree rings. When the Sun is magnetically active, faster solar wind sweeps away cosmic particles, reducing the deposition of these isotopes on Earth. Therefore, higher concentrations of these radio-isotopes in certain periods in the past indicate times of weaker solar activity and vice-versa. Harnessing this causal correlation, modern science in the 21st century has invented sophisticated techniques to reconstruct the Sun's magnetic cycles, tracing them back over the past 11,000 years [3]. Maunder minimum was not the only time when the Sun went silent; the reconstruction says our host star may have undergone such grand minimum phases for more than 20 times in the past 11 millennia!)
/Alt ()
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [0]
>>
